WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.639
At a construction site, an engineer samples cement from a truck pouring a slab.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.759
Three concrete cylinders are made and sent to a lab for compressive strength testing 28 days later.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.559
Each cylinder is 6 inches in diameter and 12 inches high.

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:28.480
When tested, the axial compressive failure load in pounds for the cylinders is 53,667, 52,166,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:39.200
and 64,918 respectively. What is the average 28 day compressive strength of the cement in PSI?

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.760
Okay, so in this problem, we're being asked to find the compressive strength in PSI.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:53.040
We can see that the axial compressive failure load of the cylinders is provided in pounds

00:00:53.839 --> 00:00:59.679
and we are given the dimensions of each concrete cylinder in inches.

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:09.680
So the manual here, if you want to search for something to look at, you can use the term compressive or

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:18.159
FC if you're using the search command. And that can bring you to chapter 2, section 2.5.1,

00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:25.359
material properties and testing. This is a section about compressive strength

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:29.840
if you need a refresher on it, but the section itself does not provide an equation to use.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.560
Instead, the only thing it notes that I think is worth pointing out here is a strength test

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:44.000
shall be the average of the strengths of at least 2, 6 by 12 inch cylinders. We have been

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:50.959
provided with three cylinders. So we are looking at units of PSI and as mentioned, we've been given

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.960
the compressive load in pounds and the cylinder dimensions in inches. All we need to do is

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.240
calculate the compressive strength of each sample and then find the result. So let's get started.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:11.599
The area equation is going to be needed so that we can calculate the area of our concrete cylinder.

00:02:12.479 --> 00:02:18.159
So each cylinder is 6 inches in diameter and an axial compressive test

00:02:20.799 --> 00:02:30.639
is applied to the y-axis in a cylinder. Therefore, we're going to be using the 6 inches and not

00:02:30.719 --> 00:02:43.919
the 12 inches. So area in this case is going to be pi d squared over 4. In this case, that's going to

00:02:43.919 --> 00:03:00.000
be pi times 6 inches squared over 4. And this is going to come out to about 28.26 inches squared.

00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:08.879
Now all we have to do is divide each of the axial compressive failure loads by that number.

00:03:09.759 --> 00:03:30.000
So 53, 6, 6, 7 pounds over 28.26 inches squared equals about 18, 99 pound over inches.

00:03:30.799 --> 00:03:48.799
Geward also called PSI. Then the second cylinder is 52, 116 pounds over 28.26 inches squared.

00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:59.599
That's going to come out to about 18, 45 pound per inch squared. And then finally, we have

00:04:01.199 --> 00:04:16.399
64, 9, 1, 8 pound over 28.26 inches squared. And that's going to come out to about 22, 97

00:04:17.599 --> 00:04:24.000
pound over inches squared. Now all we have to do is get the average of these values. So

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:46.160
18, 99 plus 18, 45 plus 2, 2, 9, 7 divided by 3 is going to give us 2013.67 pound over inches

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:53.680
squared, which is very close to our answer of C. And that's all it takes to solve this particular

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:54.879
problem.
